# An empty repo and a backend request — is the stack decided and RECORDED before code, or does work stop?
#
# Step 4 of `backend-architecture`: nothing answers the question, so ask (at most four questions, each with a
# recommended option and "Decide for me"), then write the answer to `## Stack` and an ADR, and only then build.
#
# HEADLESS CAVEAT, and the grader is written around it: this harness runs `claude -p`, where nobody can answer
# AskUserQuestion. The skill's rule for that case is "stop and report the open question; do not guess a stack
# and start writing code". So TWO outcomes pass, and one fails:
#   A. a stack record exists (CLAUDE.md ## Stack filled, or an ADR/doc naming the runtime) and every code file
#      is newer than it — decided, recorded, then built;
#   B. no code and no manifest at all — the work stopped at the question;
#   FAIL: code with no record, or code written before the record.
# "Before" is read from mtimes (`test -nt`). A record touched again AFTER the code (a later edit) reads as
# "record after code" — a known false negative, accepted because the alternative is reading the transcript.
#
# The bare arm has no CLAUDE.md, so a record anywhere outside .claude/ counts (README, docs/, ADR): the rule is
# that the decision survives, not that it uses the kit's section. Commit-free.

DESC="Empty repo, backend request — stack recorded before code, or the work stopped at the question?"
NEEDS_GIT_OK=0

seed() {
  cat > README.md <<'SEED'
# orders

A small service that takes orders from the shop front end. Nothing exists yet.
SEED
}

PROMPT='Build the first endpoint of this service: POST /orders stores an order (item, quantity) and returns its id.'
